{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/ManualResetEvent.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/ManualResetEvent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"manual reset event manual reset event","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/ManualResetEvent.md","fileName":"ManualResetEvent.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"ManualResetEvent","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Synchronization and Thread Coordination]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Synchronization and Thread Coordination","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/ManualResetEvent.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/ManualResetEvent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"manual reset event manual reset event","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/ManualResetEvent.md","fileName":"ManualResetEvent.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"ManualResetEvent","heading":"ManualResetEvent","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A `ManualResetEvent` acts as a gate: once signaled, it allows all current and later waiters to pass until code explicitly resets it. It is useful for representing a shared condition such as readiness or availability that remains true for a period.\n\nResetting at the wrong time can strand participants or let them pass under a stale condition. `ManualResetEventSlim` avoids a kernel wait handle on its cheaper paths and is appropriate for short in-process waits.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Synchronization and Thread Coordination","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/ManualResetEvent.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/ManualResetEvent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"manual reset event manu